Week a ago I posted about a very good start with a Priest, who died on the 
Medusa's level. This time the start wasn't as good, but the game ended 
better :-)

Starting with a spell of sleep helped a lot in the early game. Sokoban 
yielded a BoH and I was without reflection 'till I got a shield of 
reflection (and levitation boots) from Perseus' statue. I even got lucky 
with a /Death missing me in the Ludious and that saved my "LS.

Went down to the Castle, where a ki-rin disintegrated my helmet, had to 
turn back without MR. Now that I had reflection, I went to Ludious, since 
ESP showed that there were two gray dragons, one of those left scales 
after couple /UT zaps.

Smoky healing gave a wish, which I used for ring of free action. Other 
choices to use that wish on were "Refl, +ID or MKoT. A little later I 
found the amulet of reflection. After this I completed the Quest. Nalzok 
was propably one of the easiest nemesis I've met. Maybe the Lame One is of 
the same level.

After clearing the Castle I wished for Mjollnir. I had already seen two 
artifacts (Mitre and Sunsword an angel had in the Castle), so I had some 
doubts if I could get it. It's been a _long_ time since I last wished for 
an artifact. Actually, I think (can't check at the moment) this is the 
only ascension where I've wished for an artifact.

Then I must have done some kind of a record, from unskilled to expert in 
about 28 minutes (emptied several /CM on Quest Home level). This includes 
enchanting the Mjollnir and traveling up to the Quest Home level. I was 
lvl 23, so I hardly could miss and the zombies were easy targets to kill.

Gehennom was uneventful, just the normal hunting of the Demons. Vlad was a 
easy to kill, as always.

On dlvl 1 I made the last check; had to wish for another magic marker to 
write two more ?GD and a ?Charging, check the amulet... Amulet?! Oh bloody 
Gehennom, where is it? It turned out that I forgot to pick it up after 
pumping up Haste self on dlvl 9. Okay, then to the planes.

Earth: Rodney made his last appearance, /Death'd him.

